theHarvester on Parrot Echo
==========================

This packaging targets upstream 4.11.1, the latest GitHub release checked when
preparing it. The upstream import MUST be done before building: changing only
debian/changelog does not update the application. build-venv.py checks this.

Runtime layout
--------------

/usr/lib/theharvester/venv uses /usr/bin/python3.13 and system site-packages.
The public launchers use Python's -I mode so user-site packages and PYTHONPATH
cannot override the packaged dependencies. Nothing is installed into the global
Python environment. Pip and activation scripts are not shipped in the venv.

Use APT for every dependency except:
  aiomultiprocess 0.9.1  - not packaged in Echo
  slowapi 0.1.9         - not packaged in Echo
  playwright 1.60.0     - not packaged in Echo
  greenlet 3.3.0        - Playwright needs >=3.1.1; Echo has 3.1.0

These versions follow upstream's 4.11.1 uv.lock. Pyee and Limits, including their
transitive dependencies, come from APT. The distro Node.js replaces Playwright's
bundled Node executable. Both screenshot launch paths use /usr/bin/chromium.
There are no pip, uv or browser downloads during build, install, or normal use.

The Python package metadata patch reflects the supported Echo dependency set.
check-runtime.py checks private-to-private and private-to-system requirements;
APT remains responsible for system-to-system constraints (distro Python metadata
sometimes retains upstream upper bounds that the distro no longer enforces).
The binary includes runtime-versions.json, vendor-lock.json and vendor licenses
under /usr/share/doc/theharvester for auditing.

Preparing the initial import
----------------------------

Run from the packaging checkout, with git-buildpackage and pristine-tar on the
maintainer's machine. Fetching is a separate, network-enabled preparation step:

    python3 debian/vendor.py fetch --output-dir ..

This produces these two archives, verifying every download against SHA256:

    ../theharvester_4.11.1.orig.tar.gz
    ../theharvester_4.11.1.orig-vendor.tar.gz

The vendor component contains wheels for both supported architectures and the
published sdists for aiomultiprocess, SlowAPI and Greenlet. Playwright distributes
wheels, including its JavaScript driver and license notices, but no PyPI sdist.
Its source repositories are https://github.com/microsoft/playwright-python and
https://github.com/microsoft/playwright (tag v1.60.0).

The current upstream branch initially contains 4.10.1. Preserve the pending
Debian changes while importing the new upstream and vendor component:

    git stash push --include-untracked -m 'theHarvester venv packaging' -- debian
    gbp import-orig --upstream-version=4.11.1 --component=vendor \
        ../theharvester_4.11.1.orig.tar.gz
    git stash pop

Do not discard the stash if applying it reports a conflict. This workflow also
preserves the previously pending 4.10.1-0parrot3 changelog/control changes.
Review the resulting diff, then commit the packaging changes:

    git add debian
    git commit -m 'Package theHarvester 4.11.1 with private missing dependencies'

The vendor component is imported into upstream/vendor and tracked by
pristine-tar, just like the main orig archive. debian/gbp.conf declares the
component for subsequent exports. Do not commit hand-generated venvs or download
new dependency versions from debian/rules.

Build and test
--------------

Export a source package, then build it in a clean native Echo chroot. -d below
only skips dependency checks on the SOURCE-export machine; sbuild must install
and check all declared build dependencies normally.

    gbp buildpackage --git-export-dir=../build-area -S -us -uc -d
    cd ../build-area
    sbuild -d echo -c echo-amd64-sbuild theharvester_4.11.1-0parrot1.dsc
    autopkgtest theharvester_4.11.1-0parrot1_amd64.changes \
        -- schroot echo-amd64-sbuild

Replace the schroot names with those from your infrastructure. Repeat natively
for arm64 before publishing that architecture. The binary is NOT Architecture:
all: Greenlet contains a CPython extension, and the vendor set currently covers
CPython 3.13 on amd64 and arm64 only. Dependencies intentionally block a Python
3.14 transition until the package has been rebuilt with an updated vendor set.

The build assembles the venv offline, checks the dependency closure and CLI/API
entry points, starts the system-Node Playwright driver, and runs deterministic
upstream tests against the installed package outside the source tree.
DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS=nocheck skips the unit suite, but not dependency/startup checks.
Autopkgtest additionally tests rate limiting, authentication failure, SQLite,
multiprocessing and a real screenshot of a loopback HTTP page with an empty
Playwright browser cache. No external target or API credentials are required.

Upstream tests that contact public services are excluded explicitly. One security
test that launches real DNS and engine queries is also deselected. Functional
compatibility still needs monitoring as external services change.

Conffiles in /etc/theHarvester are managed normally by dpkg and survive upgrades.
The default YAML files and wordlists also remain in the application's data/
directory inside the venv. Old, dangling dist-packages symlinks are not shipped.

Maintenance
-----------

Keep the four vendored libraries on the distribution's security-update radar;
APT updates their system dependencies, but not these private copies. A Chromium
update must pass the screenshot test before promotion: Playwright cannot promise
compatibility with every future system browser version.

For an upstream update, review its API changes and lockfile, refresh the explicit
artifact URLs/hashes in vendor-lock.json, update the metadata patch, changelog
and tests, and import both orig components. Never update hashes automatically
when a download fails verification. For vendor-only changes to an already
published upstream version, use a new source version/orig naming scheme (for
example an incremented +ds suffix); do not replace a published orig tarball.
